From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: other processes' CPU times, and cycle counts, are real

GetProcessTimes of another process gave the caller's own user and kernel
time (ProcessTimes read times() whatever the handle); QueryProcessCycleTime
was always 0 (ProcessCycleTime stub, ~900 hits in the logs, Chrome and
Edge); QueryThreadCycleTime failed with ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED and
ThreadCycleTime was not there at all -- Chrome's per-thread CPU accounting
(ThreadTicks) is built on it.

Another process's times are read from /proc for its unix pid, found from
one of its threads, or from the process list when the handle may not list
threads. Cycle counts are the CPU time in TSC cycles: the nominal rate
from cpufreq's base_frequency, else the TSC measured against the
monotonic clock once (programs convert cycles back with their own TSC
calibration). The current thread and process use the CPU-time clocks
(nanoseconds) rather than /proc's ticks. The idle cycle times use the same
rate when base_frequency is missing (AMD, VMs), where they were 0.

Wine's kernel32 process and thread tests: no new failures.

Gate: test/cputime-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit). Mutants
SG_MUTANT_PROCTIMES_SELF (process.c), SG_MUTANT_NO_THREAD_CYCLES
(thread.c), SG_MUTANT_NO_TSC_FREQ (system.c).
---
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/thread.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/thread.c
@@ -399,10 +399,12 @@ BOOL WINAPI DECLSPEC_HOTPATCH QueueUserAPC2( PAPCFUNC func, HANDLE thread, ULONG
  */
 BOOL WINAPI DECLSPEC_HOTPATCH QueryThreadCycleTime( HANDLE thread, ULONG64 *cycle )
 {
-    static int once;
-    if (!once++) FIXME( "(%p,%p): stub!\n", thread, cycle );
-    SetLastError( ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED );
-    return FALSE;
+    THREAD_CYCLE_TIME_INFORMATION time;
+
+    if (!set_ntstatus( NtQueryInformationThread( thread, ThreadCycleTime, &time, sizeof(time), NULL ) ))
+        return FALSE;
+    *cycle = time.AccumulatedCycles;
+    return TRUE;
 }
 
 
